          "quote": "Such inspections may be made no more than once each calendar year, at reasonable times and upon reasonable notice. Commerce One shall bear all costs and expenses of such inspection. If any such inspection discloses a shortfall or an overpayment, the appropriate party shall promptly pay the amount of such shortfall or refund such overpayment. In addition, if any such inspection reveals an underpayment of more than five percent (5%) for the period under audit, Corio shall reimburse Commerce One for the reasonable cost of the examination.",
          "rationale": "Section 4.4 is the only provision giving Commerce One inspection rights over our (Corio's) books, and it already meets the preferred position on every material term: frequency is capped at once each calendar year; inspections must be at reasonable times (i.e. no unannounced visits) and on reasonable notice; they are conducted by an independent certified public accountant who must be reasonably acceptable to us; Commerce One bears all costs unless an underpayment exceeds 5%; and the right is limited to books and records with no access to our systems or premises-level data. The only sub-element short of the preferred wording is that notice is expressed as \"reasonable notice\" rather than a fixed 30 days (fallback 15 days), and the auditor is not expressly bound by a confidentiality undertaking beyond its professional duties. Both are minor mechanics that do not defeat the fallback, so we are not redlining; we would accept a quantified notice period if the clause is reopened for other reasons.",
